# Portal

A Windows equivalent of the macOS **Blink** utility — a tray-resident monitor
and manager for local development servers and Android emulators. Portal
continuously maps listening TCP ports to their owning processes, detects the
framework behind each server, resolves the real project name, and lets you
open, restart, boot and stop everything from a compact widget-style window or
the system tray.

---

## 1. Architectural Decisions

### 1.1 Tech stack

| Layer            | Choice                                    | Why |
|------------------|-------------------------------------------|-----|
| Backend          | Rust + `sysinfo` + `windows` crate         | `sysinfo` for process-tree traversal and killing; the `windows` crate for native Win32 calls (`GetExtendedTcpTable`, PEB reads, `CreateProcessW`, registry). |
| GUI              | Tauri v2 + React + TypeScript + Tailwind   | Native-feeling, frameless widget window at ~10 MB memory footprint; a full Electron app would be 10× heavier for the same UI. |
| IPC              | Tauri commands + events                    | Typed Rust commands (`Result<T, String>`) invoked from React; a background poller emits a unified `snapshot` event every 4 s. |
| Async runtime    | tokio (via `tauri::async_runtime`)         | Async Android CLI commands with hard timeouts (`tokio::time::timeout`) and kill-on-drop. |
| Process launching| `std::process` / `CreateProcessW`          | `CREATE_NO_WINDOW` so background commands never spawn rogue console windows. |

