### 1.3 Key decisions and their rationale

**Port scanning — `GetExtendedTcpTable`, not socket probing.**
Portal queries `TCP_TABLE_OWNER_PID_LISTENER` (both `AF_INET` and `AF_INET6`).
This returns every socket in the LISTEN state *together with the owning PID*,
needs no elevation, and costs microseconds. IPv6 is included because Node dev
servers frequently bind `::` / `::1`, which never appear in the IPv4 table.
Ports present in both tables are deduplicated (IPv4 owner wins); `dwLocalPort`
is stored in network byte order and converted with `u16::from_be`.

**Process identity — `sysinfo` plus direct PEB reads.**
`sysinfo` gives the process table, parent PIDs, image paths, command lines and
kill support. However, `sysinfo` does not expose the *working directory* on
Windows, and Portal needs it for project-name resolution and framework
detection. Portal therefore reads cwd — and the exact command line — straight
from the target process PEB via ntdll's `NtQueryInformationProcess`
(`ProcessBasicInformation` → PEB+0x20 → `ProcessParameters` → offset 0x38 for
`CurrentDirectory.DosPath`, offset 0x70 for `CommandLine`). This works for
processes running as the same user and requires **no elevation**; failures
(other users, protected processes) degrade gracefully to `None`.

**Framework detection — three passes.**
1. *Binary identity* from the image file name / process name: `adb*` → ADB,
   `com.docker.backend.exe` / `docker-proxy.exe` / `vpnkit` / `wslrelay.exe` /
   `wslhost.exe` → Docker/WSL, `qemu-system-*` → Android emulator. These never
   false-positive on user code paths.
2. *Command-line evidence* with **word-boundary keyword matching** (`next`,
   `vite`, `react-scripts`, `nuxt`, `vue`, `express`, `streamlit`, `mlflow`,
   `uvicorn`, `fastapi`) so `"invite"` never matches `vite` and
   `"nextjs-proxy"` never matches `next`.
3. *Dependency evidence*: for Node processes whose command line is ambiguous
   (`node server.js`), `package.json` dependencies (`next`, `vue`, `express`,
   …) identify the framework. `vite` is a bundler, not a framework — its
   flavor (Vue vs React vs plain Vite) is picked from the project's
   dependencies.

Every runtime gets a unique brand color (Next.js, React, Vue, Vite, Express,
FastAPI, Streamlit, MLflow, Docker/WSL, ADB, Android emulator, Node, Python,
other). Next.js and Express brand black would be invisible on the dark UI, so
they are rendered as light grays.

**Project name — real project identity, not folder names.**
For the working directory of each listening process Portal reads
`package.json` (via serde_json; npm scopes stripped), then `pyproject.toml`
(`name = "…"` inside `[project]`), then `uv.lock` (first `name = "…"` entry),
falling back to the directory name when none exist. Results are cached per
working directory per poll cycle.

**Restart logic — walk up, kill down, relaunch exactly.**
Frameworks spawn child processes (`pwsh` → `cmd /c` → `node npm-cli.js` →
`node <server>`), so killing the listening PID alone is not enough.

- *Root discovery*: Portal walks up the Windows parent chain (via sysinfo)
  from the listening PID. The walk stops at PID 0/4, missing parents,
  non-runner binaries (terminals, editors, anything unfamiliar), shells
  without a real command invocation (interactive `pwsh.exe` — so the walk
  never escapes into the user's terminal), or a project-directory change
  (never crosses projects). The chain is enriched with PEB cwd/cmdline before
  the walk so anchors like `cmd /d /s /c "npm run dev"` are recognized.
- *Tree kill*: a BFS over parent→children edges collects every descendant;
  they are terminated deepest-first (`TerminateProcess`) so parents cannot
  respawn children mid-flight. Portal refuses to kill PIDs ≤ 4, itself, or
  known system processes.
- *Relaunch*: the root's captured command line is reproduced byte-for-byte via
  `CreateProcessW` with `CREATE_NO_WINDOW`, in the captured working directory.
  The child is fully detached (all handles dropped), so servers keep running
  in the background exactly like a Blink-managed process.

**Tray & window.**
Tauri v2 with the `tray-icon` feature: a 420×650 frameless (`decorations:
false`) widget window whose header doubles as the drag handle
(`data-tauri-drag-region`). Dragging requires an explicit
`core:window:allow-start-dragging` permission — it is *not* part of
`core:window:default`, so without it the frameless window cannot be moved. Left-clicking the tray icon toggles window
visibility; right-click opens the menu (Show/Hide · Start at Login check item ·
Quit). Closing the window (✕ button or Alt+F4) hides to tray instead of
quitting. "Start at Login" persists a quoted exe path under
`HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run` (per-user, no elevation)
through `RegSetValueExW` / `RegDeleteValueW`.

**Android emulator management.**
SDK discovery order: `ANDROID_HOME` → `ANDROID_SDK_ROOT` →
`%LOCALAPPDATA%\Android\Sdk`, verified by the presence of
`platform-tools\adb.exe` and `emulator\emulator.exe`. Available AVDs come from
`emulator -list-avds` (cached ~15 s to keep the poll cheap). Running instances
are matched to AVD profiles via `adb devices` (serials `emulator-5554`…) plus
`adb -s <serial> emu avd name`; if adb itself is unusable, Portal falls back to
scanning console ports 5554–5584. Status per device: `stopped`, `offline`,
`unauthorized`, `booting` (adb attached but `sys.boot_completed` ≠ 1), or
`ready`. Lifecycle actions: boot (`emulator -avd <name>`), cold boot
(`-no-snapshot-load`), and graceful stop (`adb -s <serial> emu kill`). All CLI
calls run with `CREATE_NO_WINDOW`, hard timeouts, and kill-on-drop. AVD names
are validated (alphanumeric/`-`/`_`/`.`/space/parens) so a crafted name can
never smuggle extra flags into the emulator invocation.

**Performance.**
The poll loop runs every 4 s on tokio's background runtime with
`MissedTickBehavior::Delay`: one TCP-table query (µs), one sysinfo process
refresh (~10–30 ms), PEB reads only for *unique listening* PIDs (µs each),
three tiny CLI calls only when an Android SDK exists, and `emulator
-list-avds` at most every ~15 s. Idle CPU stays far below 1%.

**Security.**
No elevation anywhere: the TCP tables, PEB reads (same-user only) and HKCU
registry writes all work as a normal user. The IPC surface validates inputs —
`open_url` accepts only localhost URLs, AVD names are character-restricted,
emulator kills require `emulator-*` serials — and restart refuses system
processes and Portal itself. The frontend CSP allows only self styles/scripts.

**Error handling.**
Every Win32 call is checked (`Result`/error codes with explicit messages);
missing Android SDK environment variables surface a readable message in the
Android tab instead of failing silently; a failed TCP scan is reported in the
snapshot's `error` field; IPC commands return `Result<T, String>` so failures
appear as toasts in the UI.

